<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>

<section xml:id="page6-equivalent-games-critique" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">
  <title>Equivalent Games, Software, and a Critique</title>

  <p>
    We'll wrap up Chapter 5 with three short sections. We aren't going to go
    in depth with these sections, so just read and think about the ideas 🙂
  </p>

  <assemblage xml:id="read-5-9-to-5-11">
    <title>Sections 5.9 through 5.11</title>
    <p>
      <alert>Read Sections 5.9, 5.10, and 5.11.</alert>
    </p>
    <p>
      Here's what's in each one:
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            <alert>5.9, Equivalent Games:</alert> how much the actual payoff
            numbers matter. Some changes to the payoffs don't change the
            analysis at all, and which changes are allowed depends on
            whether you're working with pure strategies or mixed strategies.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <alert>5.10, Software:</alert> a summary of the procedure for
            finding mixed strategy Nash equilibria.
          </p>
        </li>
        <li>
          <p>
            <alert>5.11, Critique:</alert> some reasons to question whether
            people really play mixed strategy Nash equilibria. Think about
            whether you find these arguments convincing!
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </assemblage>

  <interactive xml:id="question-form-5-9" iframe="question-form.html?section=5.9" width="100%" aspect="2:1"/>
</section>